  2787'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2788'                         EXADDR:
  2788'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2789'   32 2DD5'              	STA	SRNGF
  278C'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  278D'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  278F'   CA 27B8'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  2792'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  2793'   CD 27CD'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2796'   DA 27B6'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2799'   22 2DD6'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  279C'   22 2DD8'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  279F'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  27A0'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  27A2'   C2 27AF'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  27A5'   13                    	INX	D
  27A6'   CD 27CD'              	CALL	EXNUM
  27A9'   DA 27B6'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  27AC'   22 2DD8'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  27AF'                         EXA_YS:
  27AF'   EB                    	XCHG
  27B0'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


  27B2'   32 2DD5'              	STA	SRNGF
  27B5'   C9                    	RET
  27B6'                         EXA_NO:
  27B6'   EB                    	XCHG
  27B7'   C9                    	RET
  27B8'                         EXA_AL:
  27B8'   23                    	INX	H
  27B9'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27BA'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  27BD'   22 2DD6'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  27C0'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  27C3'   22 2DD8'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  27C6'   E1                    	POP	H
  27C7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27C9'   32 2DD5'              	STA	SRNGF
  27CC'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  27CD'                         EXNUM:
  27CD'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  27CE'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  27D0'   D8                    	RC
  27D1'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  27D3'   3F                    	CMC
  27D4'   D8                    	RC
  27D5'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  27D8'                         EXN_LP:
  27D8'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  27D9'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  27DB'   DA 27F5'              	JC	EXN_DN
  27DE'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  27E0'   D2 27F5'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  27E3'   13                    	INX	D
  27E4'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  27E5'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  27E6'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  27E8'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  27E9'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  27EA'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  27EB'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  27EC'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  27ED'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  27EE'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  27EF'   D1                    	POP	D
  27F0'   09                    	DAD	B
  27F1'   C1                    	POP	B
  27F2'   C3 27D8'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  27F5'                         EXN_DN:
  27F5'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  27F6'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  27F7'                         EX_S:
  27F7'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27F8'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  27F9'   CA 283B'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  27FC'   32 2DDA'              	STA	SDELIM
  27FF'   23                    	INX	H
  2800'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2803'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2806'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2807'   CD 2841'              	CALL	SCOPY
  280A'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  280B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  280C'   CA 283B'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  280F'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  2812'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2814'   32 2DA2'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  2817'   11 2DEC'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  281A'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  281C'   CD 2841'              	CALL	SCOPY
  281F'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2820'   32 2DEB'              	STA	SREPLN
  2823'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2824'   32 2DDB'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2827'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2828'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  282A'   C2 2833'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  282D'   23                    	INX	H
  282E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2830'   32 2DDB'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2833'                         EX_SG:
  2833'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2834'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2835'   C2 25C2'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2838'   C3 2861'              	JMP	SUBGO
  283B'                         SUBNOP:
  283B'   21 268A'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  283E'   C3 25D0'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  2841'                         SCOPY:
  2841'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2843'                         SCP_LP:
  2843'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2844'   B9                    	CMP	C
  2845'   D2 285E'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2848'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2849'   B7                    	ORA	A
  284A'   CA 285E'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  284D'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  284E'   21 2DDA'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2851'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2852'   E1                    	POP	H
  2853'   CA 285D'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2856'   12                    	STAX	D
  2857'   13                    	INX	D
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  2858'   23                    	INX	H
  2859'   04                    	INR	B
  285A'   C3 2843'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  285D'                         SCP_SK:
  285D'   23                    	INX	H
  285E'                         SCP_DN:
  285E'   AF                    	XRA	A
  285F'   12                    	STAX	D
  2860'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  2861'                         SUBGO:
  2861'   CD 125A'              	CALL	GZONE
  2864'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2867'   22 2DE9'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  286A'   21 2DE6'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  286D'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  2870'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2871'   32 2DDC'              	STA	SANY
  2874'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2877'   22 2DDE'              	SHLD	SBACK
  287A'   3A 2DD5'              	LDA	SRNGF
  287D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  287E'   CA 2890'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  2881'   CD 2150'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  2884'   DA 290C'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2887'   2A 2DD6'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  288A'   CD 11C8'              	CALL	GOLN
  288D'   C3 2896'              	JMP	SUBLP
  2890'                         SUB_1L:
  2890'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2893'   22 2DE0'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2896'                         SUBLP:
  2896'   CD 2912'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2899'   B7                    	ORA	A
  289A'   CA 28A8'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  289D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  289F'   32 2DDC'              	STA	SANY
  28A2'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  28A5'   22 2DDE'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  28A8'                         SUB_NX:
  28A8'   2A 2DE0'              	LHLD	SLINES
  28AB'   2B                    	DCX	H
  28AC'   22 2DE0'              	SHLD	SLINES
  28AF'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  28B0'   B5                    	ORA	L
  28B1'   CA 28CF'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  28B4'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  28B7'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28B8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  28BB'   3A 2DDC'              	LDA	SANY
  28BE'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28BF'   CA 28C9'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


  28C2'   2A 2DDE'              	LHLD	SBACK
  28C5'   23                    	INX	H
  28C6'   22 2DDE'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  28C9'                         SUB_AE:
  28C9'   CD 0A63'              	CALL	ATEOF
  28CC'   D2 2896'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  28CF'                         SUBDON:
  28CF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  28D2'   3A 2DDC'              	LDA	SANY
  28D5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28D6'   CA 28F4'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  28D9'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28DB'   32 2D17'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  28DE'   2A 2DDE'              	LHLD	SBACK
  28E1'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  28E2'   B5                    	ORA	L
  28E3'   CA 28EE'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  28E6'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  28E8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  28EB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  28EE'                         SUB_PL:
  28EE'   CD 1272'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  28F1'   C3 07C1'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  28F4'                         SUBNFD:
  28F4'   21 2DE6'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  28F7'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  28FA'   2A 2DE9'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  28FD'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  2900'   21 265A'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2903'   CD 25D0'              	CALL	EXERR
  2906'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2909'   C3 25CB'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  290C'                         SUBBAD:
  290C'   21 2648'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  290F'   C3 25C5'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-127


  2912'                         SUBLIN:
  2912'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2913'   32 2DDD'              	STA	SLCHG
  2916'   CD 2991'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2919'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  291C'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  291C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  291F'   DA 2927'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  2922'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  2924'   C2 291C'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2927'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2927'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  292A'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  292B'   CD 1804'              	CALL	CEND
  292E'   D1                    	POP	D
  292F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  2932'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  2933'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2936'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2937'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2938'   FA 298A'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  293B'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  293C'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  293D'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2940'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2943'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2946'   DA 298A'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2949'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  294C'   EB                    	XCHG
  294D'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  2950'   EB                    	XCHG
  2951'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2954'   EB                    	XCHG
  2955'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2956'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2959'   D1                    	POP	D
  295A'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  295B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  295E'   3A 2DDC'              	LDA	SANY
  2961'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2962'   C2 296F'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2965'   3A 2DDD'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2968'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2969'   C2 296F'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  296C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  296F'                         SUBL_C:
  296F'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


  2972'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2973'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2975'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2978'   11 2DEB'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  297B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  297E'                         SUBL_I:
  297E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2980'   32 2DDD'              	STA	SLCHG
  2983'   3A 2DDB'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2986'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2987'   C2 2927'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  298A'                         SUBL_D:
  298A'   CD 2991'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  298D'   3A 2DDD'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2990'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2991'                         SUBBOL:
  2991'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2994'   CD 0624'              	CALL	BOL
  2997'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  299A'                         VC_SRCH:
  299A'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  299D'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  299E'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  29A1'   22 2DCB'              	SHLD	SCNT
  29A4'   C1                    	POP	B
  29A5'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  29A6'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  29A8'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  29AA'   CA 29AF'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  29AD'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  29AF'                         VS_DIR:
  29AF'   32 2DA0'              	STA	SDIR
  29B2'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  29B5'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  29B8'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  29B9'   CD 1EF9'              	CALL	RDLINE
  29BC'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29BD'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  29BE'   3A 2D77'              	LDA	RLLEN
  29C1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29C2'   CA 29D7'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  29C5'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  29C6'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  29C8'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  29CB'   19                    	DAD	D
  29CC'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  29CE'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  29CF'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  29D2'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-129


  29D4'   32 2DA2'              	STA	SHAVE
  29D7'                         VS_OLD:
  29D7'   3A 2DA2'              	LDA	SHAVE
  29DA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29DB'   CA 29E7'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  29DE'   3A 2DA0'              	LDA	SDIR
  29E1'   32 2DA1'              	STA	SDIRE
  29E4'   C3 2A10'              	JMP	SRCHN
  29E7'                         VS_NOP:
  29E7'   21 268A'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  29EA'   C3 25D0'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  29ED'                         VC_N:
  29ED'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  29F0'   3A 2DA0'              	LDA	SDIR
  29F3'   C3 29FD'              	JMP	VN_GO
  29F6'                         VCB_N:
  29F6'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  29F9'   3A 2DA0'              	LDA	SDIR
  29FC'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  29FD'                         VN_GO:
  29FD'   32 2DA1'              	STA	SDIRE
  2A00'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2A03'   22 2DCB'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A06'   3A 2DA2'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2A09'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A0A'   CA 29E7'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  2A0D'   C3 2A10'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2A10'                         SRCHN:
  2A10'   CD 125A'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2A13'   CD 06F4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
  2A16'                         SN_LP:
  2A16'   CD 2A4B'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2A19'   DA 2A36'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2A1C'   2A 2DCB'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2A1F'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2A20'   22 2DCB'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A23'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2A24'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2A25'   C2 2A16'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2A28'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2A2B'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2A2E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A30'   32 2DAD'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2A33'   C3 1272'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2A36'                         SN_BAD:
  2A36'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2A39'   DA 0749'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-130


  2A3C'   CD 073C'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2A3F'   21 265A'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2A42'   CD 25D0'              	CALL	EXERR
  2A45'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2A48'   C3 25CB'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2A4B'                         SRCHGO:
  2A4B'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A4D'   32 2DD4'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  2A50'   CD 2A66'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2A53'   D0                    	RNC
  2A54'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2A57'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2A58'   3A 2DA1'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2A5B'   2F                    	CMA
  2A5C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2A5F'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A60'   32 2DD4'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2A63'   C3 2A66'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2A66'                         SRCHW:
  2A66'   3A 2DA1'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2A69'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2A6C'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2A6F'   22 2D14'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2A72'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A73'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2A76'                         SW_LP:
  2A76'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  2A79'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2A7A'   CD 2A86'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2A7D'   D0                    	RNC
  2A7E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2A81'   D2 2A76'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2A84'   37                    	STC
  2A85'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2A86'                         SRCH1:
  2A86'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2A89'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-131


  2A8C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A8D'   FA 2AB1'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2A90'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2A93'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2A96'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2A99'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2A9A'   3A 2DD4'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2A9D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A9E'   CA 2AA7'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2AA1'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2AA2'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2AA3'   37                    	STC
  2AA4'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2AA5'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2AA6'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2AA7'                         SR1_FW:
  2AA7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2AAA'   D8                    	RC
  2AAB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2AAE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2AB1'                         SR1_BK:
  2AB1'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2AB4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2AB7'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2ABA'   D8                    	RC
  2ABB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2ABE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2AC1'   D8                    	RC
  2AC2'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2AC5'                         MSGNAM:
  2AC5'   E5                    	PUSH	H
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-132


  2AC6'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2AC8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2ACB'   E1                    	POP	H
  2ACC'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2ACD'   23                    	INX	H
  2ACE'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2ACF'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2AD1'   C2 2ADE'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2AD4'   21 26F8'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2AD7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2ADA'   E1                    	POP	H
  2ADB'   C3 2B28'              	JMP	MN_END
  2ADE'                         MN_HAS:
  2ADE'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2AE0'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2AE3'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AE4'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2AE5'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2AE6'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2AE7'   CA 2AF5'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2AEA'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2AEB'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2AED'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2AF0'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2AF2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2AF5'                         MN_NAM:
  2AF5'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AF6'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2AF7'   23                    	INX	H
  2AF8'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  2AFA'                         MN_NM:
  2AFA'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2AFB'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2AFD'   CA 2B08'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2B00'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B03'   23                    	INX	H
  2B04'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2B05'   C2 2AFA'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2B08'                         MN_EXT:
  2B08'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B09'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2B0C'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2B0D'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B0E'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B10'   CA 2B28'              	JZ	MN_END
  2B13'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2B15'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B18'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2B1A'                         MN_XL:
  2B1A'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B1B'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B1D'   CA 2B28'              	JZ	MN_END
  2B20'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B23'   23                    	INX	H
  2B24'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2B25'   C2 2B1A'              	JNZ	MN_XL
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-133


  2B28'                         MN_END:
  2B28'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2B2A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2B2D'                         MSGFLG:
  2B2D'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2B2F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B30'   CA 2B40'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2B33'   3A 2D17'              	LDA	MODF
  2B36'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B37'   CA 2B40'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2B3A'   21 2702'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2B3D'   CD 2B55'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2B40'                         MF_RO:
  2B40'   3A 2D8D'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2B43'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B44'   CA 2B4D'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2B47'   21 270D'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2B4A'   CD 2B55'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2B4D'                         MF_NEW:
  2B4D'   3A 2D8B'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2B50'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B51'   C8                    	RZ
  2B52'   21 2718'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2B55'                         MF_PUT:
  2B55'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2B56'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B57'   C2 2B63'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2B5A'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2B5C'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B5D'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2B5F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B62'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B63'                         MF_STR:
  2B63'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2B66'                         EMITW:
  2B66'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2B67'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2B68'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2B6A'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2B6D'   CD 2B8B'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B70'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2B73'   CD 2B8B'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B76'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2B79'   CD 2B8B'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B7C'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2B7F'   CD 2B8B'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B82'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-134


  2B83'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2B85'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B88'   D1                    	POP	D
  2B89'   C1                    	POP	B
  2B8A'   C9                    	RET
  2B8B'                         EWDIG:
  2B8B'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2B8D'                         EW_LP:
  2B8D'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2B8E'   93                    	SUB	E
  2B8F'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2B90'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2B91'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2B92'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2B93'   DA 2B9A'              	JC	EW_DN
  2B96'   0C                    	INR	C
  2B97'   C3 2B8D'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2B9A'                         EW_DN:
  2B9A'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2B9B'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2B9C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B9D'   C2 2BA3'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2BA0'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2BA1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BA2'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2BA3'                         EW_PR:
  2BA3'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2BA4'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2BA6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BA9'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2BAB'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2BAC'                         ISEMPT:
  2BAC'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2BAF'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2BB0'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2BB1'   C0                    	RNZ
  2BB2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2BB5'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2BB6'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2BB7'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2BB8'                         MSGCTG:
  2BB8'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2BBB'   CD 2AC5'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2BBE'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2BC0'   CD 2B2D'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2BC3'   CD 2BAC'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2BC6'   C2 2BCF'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2BC9'   21 272B'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-135


  2BCC'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2BCF'                         MG_POS:
  2BCF'   21 271E'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2BD2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BD5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2BD8'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2BD9'   CD 2B66'              	CALL	EMITW
  2BDC'   21 2725'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2BDF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BE2'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2BE5'   23                    	INX	H
  2BE6'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BE7'   CD 2B66'              	CALL	EMITW
  2BEA'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2BED'   23                    	INX	H
  2BEE'   D1                    	POP	D
  2BEF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2BF2'   C8                    	RZ
  2BF3'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BF4'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2BF6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BF9'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BFA'   C3 2B66'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2BFD'                         FILMSG:
  2BFD'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2C00'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2C02'   C8                    	RZ
  2C03'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2C06'   CD 2AC5'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C09'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2C0A'   C3 2B2D'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2C0D'                         WRMSG:
  2C0D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2C10'   CD 2AC5'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C13'   21 273D'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2C16'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2C19'                         MSGPST:
  2C19'   3A 2D8A'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2C1C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C1D'   C8                    	RZ
  2C1E'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2C1F'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2C20'   32 2D8A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2C23'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C24'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-136


  2C26'   CA 2C42'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2C29'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2C2B'   CA 2C48'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2C2E'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  2C30'   CA 2C39'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2C33'   CD 2BB8'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2C36'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C39'                         MP_BIG:
  2C39'   21 26D9'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2C3C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2C3F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C42'                         MP_FIL:
  2C42'   CD 2BFD'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2C45'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C48'                         MP_WRT:
  2C48'   CD 2C0D'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2C4B'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2C4E'                         VC_CTLG:
  2C4E'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2C50'   32 2D8A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2C53'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2C55'   32 2D18'              	STA	RINTENT
  2C58'   C3 06CB'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2C5B'                         CMDTAB:
  2C5B'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  2C5C'   2C4E'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2C5E'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2C5F'   07F9'                 	DW	VC_H
  2C61'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2C62'   0823'                 	DW	VC_L
  2C64'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2C65'   08F4'                 	DW	VC_J
  2C67'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2C68'   0906'                 	DW	VC_K
  2C6A'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2C6B'   0856'                 	DW	VC_0
  2C6D'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2C6E'   0874'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2C70'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2C71'   08B2'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2C73'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2C74'   08D6'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2C76'   77                    	DB	'w'
  2C77'   0AE5'                 	DW	VC_W
  2C79'   62                    	DB	'b'
  2C7A'   0B36'                 	DW	VC_B
  2C7C'   65                    	DB	'e'
  2C7D'   0CCC'                 	DW	VC_E
  2C7F'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2C80'   0AEC'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2C82'   42                    	DB	'B'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-137


  2C83'   0B3D'                 	DW	VCBGB
  2C85'   45                    	DB	'E'
  2C86'   0CD3'                 	DW	VCBGE
  2C88'   47                    	DB	'G'
  2C89'   1198'                 	DW	VC_G
  2C8B'   67                    	DB	'g'
  2C8C'   11EB'                 	DW	VC_LG
  2C8E'   78                    	DB	'x'
  2C8F'   17CD'                 	DW	VC_X
  2C91'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  2C92'   1F6C'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  2C94'   66                    	DB	'f'
  2C95'   021A'                 	DW	VC_FF
  2C97'   46                    	DB	'F'
  2C98'   021F'                 	DW	VC_FB
  2C9A'   74                    	DB	't'
  2C9B'   0224'                 	DW	VC_TF
  2C9D'   54                    	DB	'T'
  2C9E'   0229'                 	DW	VC_TB
  2CA0'   3B                    	DB	';'
  2CA1'   0240'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
  2CA3'   2C                    	DB	','
  2CA4'   024A'                 	DW	VC_COMMA
  2CA6'   79                    	DB	'y'
  2CA7'   1AA4'                 	DW	VC_Y
  2CA9'   59                    	DB	'Y'
  2CAA'   1AAC'                 	DW	VY_YY
  2CAC'   70                    	DB	'p'
  2CAD'   1CE2'                 	DW	VC_P
  2CAF'   50                    	DB	'P'
  2CB0'   1CF7'                 	DW	VCB_P
  2CB2'   69                    	DB	'i'
  2CB3'   1825'                 	DW	VC_I
  2CB5'   61                    	DB	'a'
  2CB6'   182B'                 	DW	VC_A
  2CB8'   41                    	DB	'A'
  2CB9'   184B'                 	DW	VCB_A
  2CBB'   49                    	DB	'I'
  2CBC'   1867'                 	DW	VCB_I
  2CBE'   72                    	DB	'r'
  2CBF'   19A9'                 	DW	VC_R
  2CC1'   52                    	DB	'R'
  2CC2'   19FD'                 	DW	VCB_R
  2CC4'   64                    	DB	'd'
  2CC5'   1A12'                 	DW	VC_D
  2CC7'   44                    	DB	'D'
  2CC8'   17B5'                 	DW	VCB_D
  2CCA'   43                    	DB	'C'
  2CCB'   17AF'                 	DW	VCB_C
  2CCD'   63                    	DB	'c'
  2CCE'   1A1A'                 	DW	VC_C
  2CD0'   6F                    	DB	'o'
  2CD1'   18B5'                 	DW	VC_O
  2CD3'   4F                    	DB	'O'
  2CD4'   18CA'                 	DW	VCB_O
  2CD6'   4A                    	DB	'J'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-138


  2CD7'   18E4'                 	DW	VCB_J
  2CD9'   7E                    	DB	'~'
  2CDA'   1966'                 	DW	VC_TILD
  2CDC'   3A                    	DB	':'
  2CDD'   1F97'                 	DW	VC_EX
  2CDF'   2F                    	DB	'/'
  2CE0'   299A'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2CE2'   3F                    	DB	'?'
  2CE3'   299A'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2CE5'   6E                    	DB	'n'
  2CE6'   29ED'                 	DW	VC_N
  2CE8'   4E                    	DB	'N'
  2CE9'   29F6'                 	DW	VCB_N
  2CEB'   48                    	DB	'H'
  2CEC'   0D90'                 	DW	VC_HIGH
  2CEE'   4D                    	DB	'M'
  2CEF'   0DA7'                 	DW	VC_MIDL
  2CF1'   4C                    	DB	'L'
  2CF2'   0DCA'                 	DW	VC_LOWL
  2CF4'   06                    	DB	06H			; ^F -- page forward
  2CF5'   0DE7'                 	DW	VC_PGDN
  2CF7'   02                    	DB	02H			; ^B -- page back
  2CF8'   0E45'                 	DW	VC_PGUP
  2CFA'   04                    	DB	04H			; ^D -- half page down
  2CFB'   0F26'                 	DW	VC_HFDN
  2CFD'   15                    	DB	15H			; ^U -- half page up
  2CFE'   0FED'                 	DW	VC_HFUP
  2D00'   0C                    	DB	0CH			; ^L -- redraw
  2D01'   1195'                 	DW	VC_REDR
  2D03'   2E                    	DB	'.'			; '.' -- repeat the last change
  2D04'   017D'                 	DW	VC_DOT
  2D06'   75                    	DB	'u'			; 'u' -- undo (and undo the undo)
  2D07'   0545'                 	DW	VC_UNDO
  2D09'   6D                    	DB	'm'		; m{a-c} -- set a mark
  2D0A'   032E'                 	DW	VC_MK
  2D0C'   60                    	DB	60H		; `{a-c} -- back to its exact spot
  2D0D'   0333'                 	DW	VC_BQ
  2D0F'   27                    	DB	27H		; '{a-c} -- back to its line's first non-blank
  2D10'   0338'                 	DW	VC_AQ
  2D12'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DATA
                                ;============================================================
  2D13'   00                    EDMODE: DB	EM_CMD
  2D14'   0000                  CMDCNT: DW	0		; count prefix (0 = none)
  2D16'   00                    QUITF:	DB	0		; set 1 by :q / :wq
  2D17'   00                    MODF:	DB	0		; 1 = unsaved changes
  2D18'   00                    RINTENT:DB	R_FULL		; render intent for the last key (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2D19'   01                    ILN:	DB	1		; lines an R_ILIN paint opens (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2D1A'   00                    ICHAPP:	DB	0		; 1 = the R_ICH char went at the row's end, so
                                				;   nothing follows it to shift: no ICH needed
  2D1B'   00                    ILABOV:	DB	0		; 1 = the row above an R_ILIN's opened rows lost
                                				;   text to them and must be redrawn too
  2D1C'   FFFE                  GOALCOL:DW	0FFFEH		; sticky goal screen column for j/k (0FFFFH = EOL, 0FFFEH = stale)
  2D1E'   00                    WBIG:	DB	0		; word motion: 0 = w/b/e, 1 = W/B/E
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-139


  2D1F'   00                    WPULL:	DB	0		; w/W ended off a line's end and was pulled back
  2D20'   00                    HFAMT:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: rows (vim's 'scroll'); 0 = NEDIT/2
  2D21'   00                    HFN:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: the rows this one goes
  2D22'   0000                  HFTP:	DW	0		; ^D/^U: -> the new top line's entry
  2D24'   00                    HTN:	DB	0		; row table: its entries
  2D25'   00                    HTCUR:	DB	0		; ... the cursor line's
  2D26'   00                    HTTP:	DB	0		; ... the top line's
  2D27'   00                    HTEOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its last is the file's last line
  2D28'   00                    HTBOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its first is the file's first
  2D29'   0000                  RWR:	DW	0		; ^F/^B: rows still to go
  2D2B'   00                    RWMV:	DB	0		; ^F/^B: 1 = the table was made again on the way
  2D2C'   00                    OVDIR:	DB	0		; OVLAP: 0 = h2.. are above h1 in the table
  2D2D'   0000                  JKCNT:	DW	0		; j/k: count
  2D2F'   0000                  JKN:	DW	0		; j/k: lines scanned for (min(count, limit))
  2D31'   0000                  JKMOV:	DW	0		; j: lines moved
  2D33'   0000                  JKBK:	DW	0		; k: scan position, bytes back from GAPBEG
  2D35'   00                    JKPHN:	DB	0		; j: 1 = moved may be one less (last LF ends the file)
  2D36'   00                    JKLST:	DB	0		; j: last byte of the scan
  2D37'   0000                  JKVC:	DW	0		; j/k landing: screen column
  2D39'   0000                  JKLS:	DW	0		; j/k landing: last content char (0FFFFH = none)
  0001                          PG	EQU	1		; pending-prefix codes held in GPEND/GSAVE
  0002                          PZ	EQU	2
  0003                          PR	EQU	3		;   ('r', whose next key is its operand)
  0004                          PM	EQU	4		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running (MKWHAT, not
  0005                          PJ	EQU	5		;   GPEND: each reads its own mark letter, so none
  0006                          PJL	EQU	6		;   of them leaves anything pending)
                                ;--- '.' (repeat): see the '.' section above for how these are driven.
  2D3B'   00                    DOTCHG: DB	0		; 1 = the command being typed changed the text
  2D3C'   01                    DOTSTA: DB	1		; 1 = the next key starts a new recording
  2D3D'   00                    DOTRPL: DB	0		; 1 = '.' is replaying, so nothing is recorded
  2D3E'   00                    DOTOVF: DB	0		; 1 = the change outgrew the scratch
  2D3F'   00                    DOTLK:	DB	0		; the last key recorded, encoded
  2D40'   0000*                 DOTSP:	DW	DOTSCR		; the scratch's write pointer
  2D42'   00                    DOTLEN: DB	0		; keys in DOTBUF (0 = nothing to repeat)
  2D43'   00                    DOTIX:	DB	0		; the replay's read index into DOTBUF
  2D44'   0000                  DOTNCT: DW	0		; the count typed on the '.' (0 = none)
                                
  2D46'   00                    GPEND:	DB	0		; pending 'g' / 'Z' / 'r' (gg / ZZ / r{char})
  2D47'   00                    GSAVE:	DB	0		; the pending snapshot for the current command
  2D48'   00                    PENDOP: DB	0		; pending operator ('d' for dd)
  2D49'   00                    PSAVE:	DB	0		; pending-op snapshot for the current command
  2D4A'   00                    DDSNG:	DB	0		; 1 = the current dd deletes a single line (DL paint)
  2D4B'   00                    OPCLSV: DB	0		; operator: motion class (0 excl, 1 incl, 2 linewise,
                                				;   3 excl but a jump -- no end-of-line clamp)
  2D4C'   00                    CWEOL:	DB	0		; operator: the motion is 'cw' rewritten to 'ce',
                                				;   and its first word is yet to be stepped
  2D4D'   0000                  OPSTA:	DW	0		; operator: span start (cursor before the motion)
  2D4F'   0000                  OPEND2: DW	0		; operator: span end (cursor after the motion)
  2D51'   00 00 00              OPSFP:	DB	0,0,0		; operator: the start as a place in the file
  2D54'   00 00 00              OPSLO:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where its line starts
  2D57'   00 00 00              OPSHI:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where the next line does (OPPGD: the span's
                                				;   high end, whichever line that turned out to be)
  2D5A'   00 00 00              OPTOGO: DB	0,0,0		; OPPGD: bytes still to take
  2D5D'   0000                  OPLO:	DW	0		; operator: low offset of the delete range
  2D5F'   0000                  OPHI:	DW	0		; operator: high offset of the delete range
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-140


  2D61'   0000                  CNTV:	DW	0		; motion repeat counter
  2D63'   0000                  TTI:	DW	0		; TAILTAB scan index
  2D65'   0000                  WKI:	DW	0		; scan index
  2D67'   0000                  WKN:	DW	0		; cached LOGLEN
  2D69'   0000                  WKTERM: DW	0		; line terminator offset
  2D6B'   0000                  WKBOL:	DW	0		; EOLC scratch: line BOL
  2D6D'   00                    WKCLS:	DB	0		; word motion: class of the current run
  2D6E'   00                    VRCH:	DB	0		; 'r' / R: the char being stored
  2D6F'   0000                  RPDEL:	DW	0		; R: chars overwritten, still in the gap (BS puts back)
  2D71'   0000                  RPAPP:	DW	0		; R: chars appended past the line end (BS deletes)
  2D73'   0000                  RPGE:	DW	0		; R: GAPEND as the last overwrite left it
                                
                                
  2D75'   0000                  RLPTR:	DW	0		; RDLINE: write pointer
  2D77'   00                    RLLEN:	DB	0		; RDLINE: chars stored
  2D78'   00                    RLMAX:	DB	0		; RDLINE: capacity
  2D79'   00                    EXOP:	DB	0		; ex: the command (EO_xx)
  2D7A'   00                    EXARG:	DB	0		; ex: 0 = no file name given
  2D7B'   00                    EXBAK:	DB	0		; ex: the write keeps a backup
  2D7C'   0000                  EXCMD:	DW	0		; ex: text shown after a message
  2D7E'   00                    WPROW:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's row
  2D7F'   00                    WPBYT:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's byte in its record
  2D80'   0000                  WPREC:	DW	0		; :w -- the cursor's record in the file
  2D82'   0000                  ZDIF:	DW	0		; G/gg: target - top (low 16 bits)
  2D84'   00                    ZLF:	DB	0		; G/gg: lines from the target up to the top
  2D85'   0000                  ZSVCNT: DW	0		; G/gg: CMDCNT around LINPSB
  2D87'   0000                  ZSVAUX: DW	0		; G/gg: AUXCNT around LINPSB
  2D89'   00                    ZSVDIR: DB	0		; G/gg: DIRFLG around LINPSB
  2D8A'   00                    MSGWHT: DB	0		; the message the bottom row still owes (MW_xxx)
  2D8B'   00                    MSGNEW: DB	0		; the file was not there when it was opened ('[New]')
  2D8C'   00                    EXBANG: DB	0		; ex: 0 = '!' given
  2D8D'   00                    ROFLAG: DB	0		; command line: '-R' / '/R' was given
  2D8E'   00                    PLUSF:	DB	0		; command line: '+' was given
  2D8F'   00                    YMODF:	DB	0		; yy: MODF across the delete + put back
  2D90'   00                    YDCHG:	DB	0		; yy: DOTCHG across it too (see '.')
  2D91'   00                    YURPL:	DB	0		; yy: UNDRPL across it too (see 'u')
  2D92'   0000                  YOFF:	DW	0		; yy: the cursor, which a yank leaves alone
  2D94'   0000                  YPT:	DW	0		; yy: where the lines came from
  2D96'   0000                  PCNT:	DW	0		; p/P: copies still to put
  2D98'   00 00 00              PPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; p/P: the first line put, a file position
  2D9B'   0000                  PLFS:	DW	0		; p/P: the lines in the register
  2D9D'   00                    PCPY:	DB	0		; p/P: the copies, kept for the paint
  2D9E'   0000                  YLDLT:	DW	0		; yy: LNDLT across the delete + put back
                                
  2DA0'   00                    SDIR:	DB	0		; the last '/' or '?' direction (FFH = back)
  2DA1'   00                    SDIRE:	DB	0		; ... the direction THIS search runs in ('N')
  2DA2'   00                    SHAVE:	DB	0		; nonzero once a pattern has been entered
  2DA3'   00                    FNDKND: DB	0		; f/F/t/T: which find ';' repeats (0 = none yet)
  2DA4'   00                    FNDCH:	DB	0		; ... and the character it looked for
  2DA5'   00                    FNDRUN: DB	0		; the find THIS command is running (',' reverses)
  2DA6'   00                    FNDSTP: DB	0		; how far along it starts: 2 for a 't' repeat
  2DA7'   0000                  FNDN:	DW	0		; occurrences still to count off
  2DA9'   0000                  FNDCUR: DW	0		; the cursor it started from
  2DAB'   0000                  FNDLIM: DW	0		; ... and the end of the line it may not pass
  2DAD'   00                    GPKEEP: DB	0		; GPLACE: keep the column (a search) not FNBLNK
  2DAE'   00 00 00              MKVAL:	DB	0,0,0		; marks: nonzero once 'm' has set that one.  It
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-141


                                				;   lives HERE and not in MKTAB because the image
                                				;   guarantees a DB, and nothing guarantees a DS
  2DB1'   00 00 00              MKEDIT: DB	0,0,0		; MKSHFT: where in the file the edit is
  2DB4'   0000                  MKN:	DW	0		; ... how long it is
  2DB6'   00                    MKTYP:	DB	0		; ... and which way: 1 text went in, 2 came out
  2DB7'   00 00 00              MKPOS:	DB	0,0,0		; MKJMP: the file position being jumped to
  2DBA'   00                    MKWHAT: DB	0		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running
  2DBB'   00                    MKHOLD: DB	0		; set across YKGO's delete + put back (a yank
                                				;   that could not copy): MKSHFT must leave the
                                				;   marks alone through it
  2DBC'   0000                  YACT:	DW	0		; how this yank takes its lines (VD_DD / YSPAN)
  2DBE'   0000                  YTGT:	DW	0		; YSPAN: the text's length once the span is out
  2DC0'   0000                  YWAS:	DW	0		; ... and its length BEFORE the lines came out
  2DC2'   0000                  YCOL:	DW	0		; ... how far into them the cursor sat
  2DC4'   00 00 00              YPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; ... and where in the file they start
                                ;--- the yank that copies instead (YNCOPY / YNSPAN) ---
  2DC7'   0000                  YNPG:	DW	0		; the window the yank started in (YNDONE's paint)
  2DC9'   0000                  YNCNT:	DW	0		; YNLINE: the lines it still wants
  2DCB'   0000                  SCNT:	DW	0		; searches still to do (the count)
  2DCD'   00 00 00              SOFF:	DB	0,0,0		; BRKON: where in the FILE the command began
  2DD0'   0000                  SLDLT:	DW	0		; ... and LNDLT: for a search that fails, and
                                				;   any move an ESC stops
  2DD2'   00                    BRKARM: DB	0		; nonzero: CNTTST looks for an ESC
  2DD3'   00                    BRKHIT: DB	0		; nonzero: one was found, and the count spent
  2DD4'   00                    SSKIP:	DB	0		; skip the character under the cursor (not
                                				;   on the wrapped pass)
                                
  0024                          SREPMX	EQU	EXMAX-4		; ':s' -- bounded by the ex line itself, so
                                				;   SCOPY can never truncate a line anyone is
                                				;   able to type: the shortest possible
                                				;   substitute is 's' '/' + one pattern char +
                                				;   '/' before the replacement starts, and
                                				;   RDLINE stores at most EXMAX.  Tied to
                                				;   EXMAX rather than written out, the way
                                				;   BUF.MAC ties SRCMAX to SRMAX, so the two
                                				;   cannot drift apart.
  2DD5'   00                    SRNGF:	DB	0		; ex: a line range was given
  2DD6'   0000                  SRNG1:	DW	0		; ex: its first line
  2DD8'   0000                  SRNG2:	DW	0		; ... and its last (FFFFH = the file's last)
  2DDA'   00                    SDELIM: DB	0		; ':s' the delimiter typed
  2DDB'   00                    SGFLAG: DB	0		; ':s' the 'g' flag
  2DDC'   00                    SANY:	DB	0		; ':s' something in the range has changed
  2DDD'   00                    SLCHG:	DB	0		; ':s' this line changed
  2DDE'   0000                  SBACK:	DW	0		; ':s' lines walked past the last change
  2DE0'   0000                  SLINES: DW	0		; ':s' / ':N,Mw' lines of the range still to do
  2DE2'   00                    RWPTR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' the next free byte of the record at
                                				;   0080H, as that address's low byte
  2DE3'   00                    RWERR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' a record would not go: the disk is full
  2DE4'   00                    RDANY:	DB	0		; ':r' something has been read in
  2DE5'   00                    RDBRK:	DB	0		; ':r' the line above wants a line end first
  2DE6'   00 00 00              SOFFS:	DB	0,0,0		; ':s' the cursor's place in the file, for one that
  2DE9'   0000                  SLDLTS: DW	0		;   changes nothing, and its LNDLT
  2DEB'   00                    SREPLN: DB	0		; ':s' the replacement's length, kept
  2DEC'                         SREPL:	DS	SREPMX+1	;   just below it: WM's RDSTR layout,
                                				;   so INSSTR can splice it
                                
